WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:10.260
CHARLES NESSON: Who am I to be doing this enterprise with you?

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.470
Why am I doing it?

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:15.480
What's my goal?

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:22.300
Well, I'm the founder of the Berkman Center for Internet and Society

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:31.340
at Harvard, soon to be the Berkman Klein Center, but that's another story.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:39.580
The Berkman Center goes back to a basic vision of cyberspace

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:45.060
as tripartite in its power structure.

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:52.550
Three huge forces-- the force of states of nations--

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:56.670
the whole idea of traditional law as we've

00:00:56.670 --> 00:01:04.349
understood it-- the force of corporate power, market,

00:01:04.349 --> 00:01:14.240
extraordinarily powerful economics, and the force of University-- of what you

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:23.480
might think of as ethics, of truth, of the idea that, well, the Socratic idea,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:34.690
that if we combine our diversity, if we join it and learn from each other,

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:41.990
all we have to teach each other, then we have a force.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:48.160
And you can see that it's been a force down through the ages.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:55.710
But here, in its way suddenly, at the advent

00:01:55.710 --> 00:02:00.670
of the internet, the idea that suddenly we

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:07.370
are universal in our connection in a way we've never been before,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:15.320
we find ourselves in a new environment, hostile in many respects,

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:24.950
interesting, intriguing, but altogether a shift from an orientation that

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:32.990
allows us to be individual to one in which we live in a connected space.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:39.420
I'm a professor of law, law in the sense of process,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:49.000
law in the sense of Socrates, law in the sense of modes of behavior

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:51.710
that allow us to get along.

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:58.940
And I believe that the number one question posed by internet

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:02.110
is one of governance.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:08.020
Not the question, how do we govern the internet?

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:16.050
That question, if you understand its dimensions, doesn't have an answer.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:22.590
But the question rather is how do we recognize that we as individuals

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:29.880
and as institutions, as universities, as states, as corporations,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:33.700
recognize that we are in a new environment?

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:41.760
And in a new environment, are challenged to learn how to live and thrive in it.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:48.810
In that environment, the question that we face is, how do we govern ourselves?

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:52.960
And the approach that I would like to follow with you

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:58.350
draws its inspiration from the American jury

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:03.300
and from poker, two of my favorite subjects.

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:09.960
But both with the element in common that people sit around a table,

00:04:09.960 --> 00:04:16.529
and they play as equal players in the rhetorical game that goes

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:21.399
back and forth to come to a decision.

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:26.760
So I invite you to come, play rhetorical poker with me,

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:33.060
learn about the original conception of the American jury--

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:38.860
not necessarily where it's come, I'm not teaching you a technical law class--

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:50.630
But the idea of discourse, the idea of listening as a means of persuading.

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:56.800
These are the skills that I would like to see us practice and share.

